irsa workimng
-----------------------

“IRSA stands for IAM Roles for Service Accounts. It allows a Kubernetes Pod running on EKS to obtain temporary AWS credentials without storing static AWS access keys.

First, the EKS cluster has an OIDC issuer. We create an IAM role whose trust policy trusts that EKS OIDC provider and restricts which Kubernetes ServiceAccount is allowed to assume the role.

Then we create a Kubernetes ServiceAccount and associate it with the IAM role. When a Pod uses that ServiceAccount, a projected Kubernetes service-account token is made available to the Pod.

When the AWS SDK inside the Pod needs credentials, it sends that token to AWS STS using `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity`.

STS validates the token against the trusted EKS OIDC provider and checks the IAM role's trust policy. If everything matches, STS returns temporary AWS credentials.

The application can then use those temporary credentials to access AWS services according to the IAM policies attached to that role.

So the flow is:

Kubernetes ServiceAccount → projected token → AWS STS → validate OIDC and trust policy → temporary AWS credentials → AWS service.”



